  "source": "IMF STA BOP; IMF macro framework; UN Comtrade",
  "sources": [
   "IMF STA BOP via KIR_BOP.xlsx sheet 'IN_STA_USD (Analytical)' rows 6 (KIR.CD_T.G.USD.*), 7 (KIR.DB_T.G.USD.*), 14 (KIR.CD_T.D45.USD.*), USD millions",
   "Nominal GDP in USD: KIR_MT.xlsx sheet 'OUT_MT', series 826NGDP_USD ('GDP - In US Dollars', Million), through 2025 (2025 = estimate)",
   "UN Comtrade monthly HS6, both sides, months Kiribati reported: /home/claude/kir-tracker/_inputs/KiriTrade/data/interim/kiribati/mirror_metrics.parquet",
   "Partner annual-return totals (all reporters): data/out/screen_applicability_3_by_year.csv (Kiribati rows) and data/out/kiribati/fish/fish_mirror_annual.csv"
  ],
  "unit": "USD millions",
  "excluded_lines": [
   {
    "period": "2024-01",
    "direction": "import",
    "hs6": "940690",
    "description": "Prefabricated buildings",
    "counterpart": "Viet Nam",
    "kiribati_value_usd_m": 3135.336,
    "partner_value_usd_m": null,
    "reason": "One line in Kiribati's return worth more than twice the country's annual GDP. It is kept out of the Kiribati-side totals shown here and reported in this list (see notes); the as-published totals are given alongside."
   }
  ],
  "notes": [
   "Kiribati's balance of payments (BOP) goods lines and Kiribati's own customs returns agree once the months Kiribati has not yet published are allowed for. The difference between Kiribati's figures and partner-reported exports is tuna that importing countries code to Kiribati because the catching vessel flies the Kiribati flag; under balance-of-payments rules much of it may correctly sit outside Kiribati's goods exports.",
   "Partner-reported imports are a lower bound (only partners that publish on time appear; China's monthly data stop at 2024-12). No trade balance is shown or should be computed from these columns.",
   "customs_*_scaled12 and partner_exports_scaled12 = value × 12 / months Kiribati reported. This is an order-of-magnitude device, not an estimate: it assumes the missing months look like the reported ones.",
   "Comtrade columns cover only the months Kiribati published a return (2017, 2018, 2020: 12 months; 2023: 7, Jun-Dec; 2024: 10; 2025: 10; 2026: 7, Jan-Jul). The partner side of the monthly dataset was pulled for those months only. Rows for 2019, 2021 and 2022 have no Kiribati monthly return (months = 0).",
   "*_annual_basis columns come from partners' ANNUAL returns to Comtrade (all 12 months, all reporters, including those that only publish annually). They are a different basis from the monthly columns and are shown so a full-year partner figure exists for every year; partner_exports_tuna_annual_basis is the fish-only annual pull (HS 03 + 1604, tuna codes).",
   "BOP goods imports for 2025 read 270.4 in the workbook copy used here (IN_STA_USD row 7); the 8 Oct pipeline run and earlier notes quoted 270.6 from a previous vintage of the same cell.",
   "2025 BOP and GDP values are estimates (marked 'Est.' in the workbook). GDP in USD is the macro framework's 826NGDP_USD series; 2026 is a projection and is not used, so pct_gdp is empty for 2026.",
   "Percent-of-GDP figures are computed here from IN_STA_USD rows and 826NGDP_USD; the Board table T4b in KIR_BOP.xlsx uses a slightly different vintage (e.g. fishing licence fees 2025: 36.1 percent there against 37.4 percent here).",
   "Tuna = HS6 030341-030349 (frozen tunas), 030487 (frozen tuna fillets) and 160414 (prepared tuna), the definition in comtrade_mirror/fish.py. Ships and aircraft = HS chapters 88 and 89.",
   "Fishing licence fees are a separate flow (BOP primary income) from the tuna series and must not be compared with it as if one explained the other.",
   "Kiribati's January 2024 import return contains one line (HS 940690 prefabricated buildings from Viet Nam, USD 3,135 million) worth about nine times Kiribati's annual GDP. It is a difference of scale, not of coverage, so it is set aside from customs_imports_kiribati (listed in meta.excluded_lines) and the as-published total is given in customs_imports_kiribati_as_published. With it set aside, 2024 customs imports scaled to 12 months (about 215) sit close to the BOP figure (226.6), as in every other year."
  ]
 },
